---

## Tools describe actions

An MCP server advertises capabilities with structured inputs. The client can discover these tools, request an action and read its result. This makes the integration boundary visible.

## Configuration belongs to the client

A local server may communicate over standard input and output. A hosted server may use an HTTP transport. Follow the selected client’s configuration format rather than copying a configuration for a different product.

## Keep credentials scoped

A server can hold a credential on behalf of a client, but that does not justify exposing every operation. Give the integration the resource and action scope the task needs.

## This project’s boundary

The documentation assistant is an HTTP endpoint, not an MCP server. Craft Anything was used to inspect the reference site; it is not a runtime dependency of this documentation application.
